When library references close a cycle, the edge backed by the fewest
references is dropped. `lib_b` references three modules of `lib_a`;
`lib_a` references one module of `lib_b`. The `lib_a -> lib_b` edge is the
weak one, so `lib_a` comes first. The warning names the reference count
and one example reference:

  $ mkdir lib_a lib_b

  $ cat > lib_a/one.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "lib_a")]]
  > [@@@css ".a-one{color:red;}"]
  > let _ = Four.x
  > EOF

  $ cat > lib_a/two.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "lib_a")]]
  > let x = 2
  > EOF

  $ cat > lib_a/three.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "lib_a")]]
  > let x = 3
  > EOF

  $ cat > lib_b/four.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.config [("library-name", "lib_b")]]
  > [@@@css ".b-four{color:blue;}"]
  > let x = (One.x, Two.x, Three.x)
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate lib_a/one.ml lib_a/two.ml lib_a/three.ml lib_b/four.ml
  styled-ppx: dependency cycle among lib_a, lib_b; dropping the edge from lib_a to lib_b (1 reference, e.g. Four in lib_a/one.ml:3) to keep a deterministic order
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .a-one{color:red;}
  .b-four{color:blue;}
